      "text": "1. First, we need to find the prime factorizations of 1407 and 903.\n   - For 1407, we start by dividing by the smallest prime number, which is 3:\n     \\[\n     1407 \\div 3 = 469\n     \\]\n     So, 1407 can be written as:\n     \\[\n     1407 = 3 \\times 469\n     \\]\n     Next, we factorize 469. Since 469 is not divisible by 2, 3, or 5, we check divisibility by 7:\n     \\[\n     469 \\div 7 = 67\n     \\]\n     So, 67 is a prime number, and we can write:\n     \\[\n     469 = 7 \\times 67\n     \\]\n     Therefore, the prime factorization of 1407 is:\n     \\[\n     1407 = 3 \\times 7 \\times 67\n     \\]\n\n   - For 903, we start by dividing by 3:\n     \\[\n     903 \\div 3 = 301\n     \\]\n     So, 903 can be written as:\n     \\[\n     903 = 3 \\times 301\n     \\]\n     Next, we check if 301 is divisible by 3. The sum of the digits of 301 is 3 + 0 + 1 = 4, which is not divisible by 3. We check divisibility by 5, and it ends in 1, so not divisible by 5. We check divisibility by 7:\n     \\[\n     301 \\div 7 = 43\n     \\]\n     So, 43 is a prime number, and we can write:\n     \\[\n     301 = 7 \\times 43\n     \\]\n     Therefore, the prime factorization of 903 is:\n     \\[\n     903 = 3 \\times 7 \\times 43\n     \\]\n\n2. From the prime factorizations, we can see that the common factors of 1407 and 903 are 3 and 7. The greatest common divisor is the product of these common factors:\n     \\[\n     \\text{GCD}(1407, 903) = 3 \\times 7 = 21\n     \\]\n\n3. So, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is:\n     \\[\n     \\boxed{21}\n     \\]",
