      "text": "To find the greatest common divisor (GCD) of 1407 and 903, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm is based on the principle that the GCD of two numbers also divides their difference. Here are the steps:\n\n1. Divide 1407 by 903 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   1407 = 903 \\cdot 1 + 504\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 504. Now, we need to find the GCD of 903 and 504.\n\n2. Divide 903 by 504 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   903 = 504 \\cdot 1 + 399\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 399. Now, we need to find the GCD of 504 and 399.\n\n3. Divide 504 by 399 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   504 = 399 \\cdot 1 + 105\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 105. Now, we need to find the GCD of 399 and 105.\n\n4. Divide 399 by 105 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   399 = 105 \\cdot 3 + 94\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 94. Now, we need to find the GCD of 105 and 94.\n\n5. Divide 105 by 94 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   105 = 94 \\cdot 1 + 11\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 11. Now, we need to find the GCD of 94 and 11.\n\n6. Divide 94 by 11 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   94 = 11 \\cdot 8 + 6\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 6. Now, we need to find the GCD of 11 and 6.\n\n7. Divide 11 by 6 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   11 = 6 \\cdot 1 + 5\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 5. Now, we need to find the GCD of 6 and 5.\n\n8. Divide 6 by 5 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   6 = 5 \\cdot 1 + 1\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 1. Now, we need to find the GCD of 5 and 1.\n\n9. Divide 5 by 1 and find the remainder.\n   \\[\n   5 = 1 \\cdot 5 + 0\n   \\]\n   So, the remainder is 0. When we reach a remainder of 0, the divisor at that step is the GCD. Therefore, the GCD of 5 and 1 is 1.\n\nThus, the greatest common divisor of 1407 and 903 is \\boxed{1}.",
